 Dominion of Some Graphs // Restore the saved color scheme preference, or // enact the browser preference if "automatic", // without expecting DOM load to have completed. // // Also restore any saved readingmode and ToC display preferences. function initializeReadingPreferences() { let saved_theme = localStorage.getItem("ar5iv_theme") || "automatic"; if (saved_theme === "automatic") { if (window.matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)").matches) { saved_theme = "dark"; } } if (saved_theme == "dark") { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-theme", "dark"); } else { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-theme", "light"); } const tocDisplay = localStorage.getItem('arxiv_html_paper_toc_display'); if (tocDisplay) { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-toc-display", tocDisplay); } const readingMode = localStorage.getItem('arxiv_html_paper_reading_mode'); if (readingMode) { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-reading-mode", readingMode); } } // Run as soon as JS starts, to minimize repainting initializeReadingPreferences(); /* Reading mode hides the chrome and the empty .arxiv-html-header marker: the pinned JS reads the marker's display to know which way to toggle. The marker draws no box. */ .arxiv-html-header { display: contents; } html[data-reading-mode="enabled"] :is(.arxiv-html-site-chrome, .arxiv-html-header, .ds-site-footer) { display: none; } /* The paper controls' icons are blocks, so they sit centred (the pinned JS shows the theme icon by making its span a block). */ .arxiv-html-site-chrome .ds-site-header-tools svg { display: block; width: 18px; height: 18px; flex-shrink: 0; fill: currentColor; } /* ar5iv dims every image in dark mode and puts it on a white wash, for paper figures; arXiv's own render as they are (the logo on the bar, the funder logos on their white chips). Important: ar5iv's dark rules are unlayered, as specific as :root:not([data-theme]) img:not(.ltx_no_dark_filter). */ :is(.ds-site-header, .ds-site-footer) img { filter: none !important; } .ds-site-header img { background-color: transparent !important; } /* Wide (the theme's 1280px): the theme lays the body out as a grid whose full-width top rows were the band's and its sticky reader bar's. The band takes the first and scrolls away; the header, the paper's bar (no site navigation, the paper's controls), takes the second and stays in view, the one sticky bar, with the anchor clearance that requires (DESIGN-POLICIES). The controls show their names. */ @media (min-width: 1280px) { html { scroll-padding-top: 80px; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome { display: contents; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome > #ds-announcement { grid-area: announce; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome > .ds-site-header { grid-area: header; position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: 3; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome .tool-icon { display: none; } } /* Narrow: the header scrolls away, and the controls show icons (names kept for screen readers; Why HTML? goes). The theme's contents panel is fixed below where its old sticky bar was, and hidden only once the reader has closed it, so until then it is an invisible box over the header and the paper: it shows only while open, under the chrome, in its place in the page. The paper's footer (build logs, reporting instructions) keeps the text's side inset; the theme gives it none, so it met the screen edges. */ @media (max-width: 1279px) { .arxiv-html-site-chrome .tool-wide { display: none; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome .tool-label { position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; overflow: hidden; clip: rect(0 0 0 0); white-space: nowrap; } html:not([data-toc-display="block"]) .ltx_page_navbar { display: none; } .ltx_page_navbar { position: absolute; top: auto; } .arxiv-html-footer { padding-inline: 1rem; } } Skip to main content Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Submit without GitHub Submit in GitHub Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Contents Reading mode Colour theme Abstract 1 Introduction 2 Dominion of Some Graphs 3 Dominion of Join of Graphs References License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2512.24115v1 [math.CO] 30 Dec 2025 Dominion of Some Graphs Julian A.D. Allagan 1 , Benkam Bobga 2 1 Department of Mathematics, Computer Science and Engineering Technology Elizabeth City State University Elizabeth City, North Carolina, U.S.A 2 Department of Mathematics University of North Georgia Gainesville, Georgia, U.S.A email: adallagan@ecsu.edu, benkam.bobga@ung.edu (January 28, 2021) Abstract Given a graph G = ( V , E ) G=(V,E) , a subset S ⊆ V S\subseteq V is a dominating set if every vertex in V ∖ S V\setminus S is adjacent to some vertex in S S . The dominating set with the least cardinality, γ \gamma , is called a γ \gamma -set which is commonly known as a minimum dominating set. The dominion of a graph G G , denoted by ζ ⁡ ( G ) \zeta(G) , is the number of its γ \gamma -sets. Some relations between these two seemingly distinct parameters are established. In particular, we present the dominions of paths, some cycles and the join of any two graphs. 1 Introduction A dominating set for a graph G = ( V , E ) G=(V,E) is a subset S ⊆ V S\subseteq V such that every vertex v ∈ V v\in V is either in S S or has a neighbor u ∈ S u\in S . The vertex u ∈ S u\in S is said to cover the vertex v ∈ V v\in V if either u = v u=v or u ​ v ∈ E uv\in E . A dominating set S S is a minimal dominating set if no proper subset S ′ ⊂ S S^{\prime}\subset S is a dominating set. The domination number γ ⁡ ( G ) \gamma(G) of a graph G G is the minimum cardinality among all dominating sets of G G . Clearly, a graph G G can have multiple minimum dominating sets. For simplicity, we refer to those sets as γ \gamma -sets . Dominating sets, in particular γ \gamma -sets, have been extensively researched. We recommend to the reader Haynes et al.’s book [ 8 ] and several other research works [ 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 , 5 , 6 , 7 , 9 ] on variants and applications of dominating sets. In this literature we found various versions of γ \gamma -set such as the perfect dominating set, the double dominating set, the strong dominating set, and the restrained dominating set. In general, each such type of γ \gamma -set has some specified properties. Here, we list five of the most commonly studied γ \gamma -sets along with their properties. Later in Example 1.1 , we illustrate each such γ \gamma -set. 1. Perfect γ \gamma -set requires that no two vertices in a γ \gamma -set cover the same vertex (outside the γ \gamma -set). 2. Connected γ \gamma -set requires that the graph induced by the γ \gamma -set be connected. 3. Total γ \gamma -set forbids isolated vertices in the subgraph induced by the γ \gamma -set. 4. Independent γ \gamma -set requires all vertices in a γ \gamma -set to be isolated vertices in the subgraph they induce. 5. clique γ \gamma set requires that the vertices in a γ \gamma -set induce a clique. Because a given graph may have multiple γ \gamma -sets, this article addresses a natural but fundamental question: How many γ \gamma -sets does a given graph have? To answer this question, we introduce the notion of dominion . The dominion (number) of a graph G = ( V , E ) G=(V,E) , denoted by ζ ⁡ ( G ) \zeta(G) , is the number of its γ \gamma -sets. In other words, ζ ⁡ ( G ) := | { S : S ​ is a dominating set in ​ G ​ and ​ | S | = γ } | \zeta(G):=|\{S:S\text{ is a dominating set in}\ G\ \text{and}\ |S|=\gamma\}| . For instance, given a complete graph G = K n G=K_{n} , it is obvious that each v ∈ V ⁡ ( G ) v\in V(G) covers the remaining n − 1 n-1 vertices of G G . Therefore ζ ⁡ ( G ) = n \zeta(G)=n while γ ⁡ ( G ) = 1 \gamma(G)=1 . Also, for a star graph G = K 1 , n G=K_{1,n} of order n + 1 ≥ 3 n+1\geq 3 , it is easy to see that γ ⁡ ( G ) = 1 = ζ ⁡ ( G ) \gamma(G)=1=\zeta(G) since only the central vertex can cover all of the remaining n ≥ 2 n\geq 2 vertices. Example 1.1 . Here, we illustrate the γ \gamma -sets of a unicyclic graph G G (see Figure 1 ). u u v v x x y y w w z z Figure 1: A sun graph G G with γ ⁡ ( G ) = 3 \gamma(G)=3 and ζ ⁡ ( G ) = 8 \zeta(G)=8 Consider the unicyclic graph G G as shown in Figure 1 . It is easy to see that γ ⁡ ( G ) = 3 \gamma(G)=3 . We list all the γ \gamma -sets with properties which are among the five commonly studied, as previously mentioned. • { u , v , w } \{u,v,w\} is a perfect γ \gamma -set, a connected γ \gamma -set, a total γ \gamma -set, and a clique γ \gamma -set. • { x , y , z } \{x,y,z\} is a perfect γ \gamma -set, and an independent γ \gamma -set. • { u , y , z } \{u,y,z\} , { v , x , z } \{v,x,z\} , and { w , x , y } \{w,x,y\} are independent γ \gamma -sets but not perfect, connected, clique or total. • { u , v , z } \{u,v,z\} , { u , w , y } \{u,w,y\} , and { v , w , x } \{v,w,x\} are γ \gamma -sets which have none of the 5 5 special properties that we previously listed. We have ζ ⁡ ( G ) = ∑ i = 0 3 ( 3 i ) = 8 \displaystyle\zeta(G)=\sum_{i=0}^{3}{3\choose i}=8 , the number of γ \gamma -sets of G G . We present a generalization of the previous example for such unicyclic graphs which are commonly known as sun graphs . Proposition 1.2 . If G G is a sun graph on 2 ​ n ≥ 6 2n\geq 6 vertices, then ζ ⁡ ( G ) = ∑ i = 0 n ( n i ) = 2 n . \displaystyle\zeta(G)=\sum_{i=0}^{n}{n\choose i}=2^{n}. Proof. For each vertex on the cycle, and for each dominating set S ⊆ V ⁡ ( G ) S\subseteq V(G) , either the vertex or its leaf neighbor must be in S S . Therefore, we can form all γ \gamma -sets S S in G G from choosing subsets of the set of n n leafs, and the cycle neighbors of unchosen leafs. This gives ∑ i = 0 n ( n i ) \displaystyle\sum_{i=0}^{n}{n\choose i} . Conversely, every S S assembled by choosing either the vertex on the cycle or the leaf, for each leaf, is a γ \gamma -set for G G . This gives ζ ⁡ ( G ) = 2 n \zeta(G)=2^{n} . ∎ 2 Dominion of Some Graphs Suppose P n := v 1 − v 2 − … − v n − 1 − v n P_{n}:=v_{1}-v_{2}-\ldots-v_{n-1}-v_{n} , denotes a path on n ≥ 2 n\geq 2 vertices. We establish each of the following claims: Claim 2.1 . There is no γ \gamma -set that includes both vertices v n − 1 , v n v_{n-1},v_{n} . Proof. Suppose n = 3 ​ k + 1 n=3k+1 . It is easy to see that γ ⁡ ( P 3 ​ k + 1 ) = k + 1 \gamma(P_{3k+1})=k+1 and without loss, let’s assume that there is a γ \gamma -set S S , with | S | = k + 1 |S|=k+1 , that includes both v 3 ​ k , v 3 ​ k + 1 v_{3k},v_{3k+1} . Since v 3 ​ k − 1 v_{3k-1} is covered by v 3 ​ k v_{3k} (and vice versa), this leaves exactly 3 ​ k − 2 3k-2 vertices of P 3 ​ k + 1 P_{3k+1} that are to be covered using the remaining k − 1 k-1 vertices of S S . Because each vertex of S S covers at most 2 2 other vertices of P 3 ​ k + 1 P_{3k+1} , k − 1 k-1 vertices can cover at most 3 ​ ( k − 1 ) 3(k-1) vertices of the path, and 3 ​ ( k − 1 ) < 3 ​ k − 2 3(k-1)<3k-2 . A similar argument addresses the case when n = 3 ​ k + 2 n=3k+2 and the case when n = 3 ​ k n=3k follows from a pigeonhole principle argument, since γ ⁡ ( P 3 ​ k ) = k \gamma(P_{3k})=k . ∎ Claim 2.2 . There is no γ \gamma -set that excludes both vertices v n − 1 , v n v_{n-1},v_{n} . Proof. Suppose S S is a γ \gamma -set of P n P_{n} that excludes both consecutive vertices v n − 1 , v n v_{n-1},v_{n} . It follows that v n ∈ V ⁡ ( P n ) v_{n}\in V(P_{n}) is left uncovered. Hence, S S is not a γ \gamma -set of P n P_{n} . ∎ Claim 2.3 . Every γ \gamma -set includes either v n − 1 v_{n-1} or v n v_{n} but not both. Claim 2.4 . When n = 3 ​ k , k ≥ 1 n=3k,k\geq 1 , ζ ⁡ ( P n ) = 1 \zeta(P_{n})=1 and the unique γ \gamma -set in P n P_{n} contains v n − 1 v_{n-1} . Moreover, when n = 3 ​ k + 2 , k ≥ 1 n=3k+2,k\geq 1 , there is only one γ \gamma -set in P n P_{n} containing v n v_{n} . Proof. When n = 3 ​ k , k ≥ 1 n=3k,k\geq 1 , we have ζ ⁡ ( P n ) = k \zeta(P_{n})=k and the conclusion follows by the pigeon-hold argument alluded in Claim 2.1 . Each of the sets of 3 3 consecutive vertices { v 3 ​ r + 1 , v 3 ​ r + 2 , v 3 ​ r + 3 } \{v_{3r+1},v_{3r+2},v_{3r+3}\} , r = 0 , … , k − 1 r=0,\ldots,k-1 , must contain at least one vertex of any γ \gamma -set S S , and since γ = k \gamma=k , each of those sets must contain exactly one element of S S . Since S S is dominating, the element of S S in { v 1 , v 2 , v 3 } \{v_{1},v_{2},v_{3}\} must be v 1 v_{1} or v 2 v_{2} . If v 1 v_{1} then the “next” element of S S must be v 4 v_{4} , which forces S = { v 1 , v 4 , … , v 3 ​ k − 2 } S=\{v_{1},v_{4},\ldots,v_{3k-2}\} , leaving v 3 ​ k v_{3k} uncovered. Therefore, the first element of S S must be v 2 v_{2} , which forces S = { v 2 , v 5 , … , v 3 ​ k − 1 , v 3 ​ k + 2 } S=\{v_{2},v_{5},\ldots,v_{3k-1},v_{3k+2}\} . Suppose that n = 3 ​ k + 2 n=3k+2 , and let S S be a γ \gamma -set in V ⁡ ( P n ) V(P_{n}) . Then | S | = k + 1 |S|=k+1 . Suppose that v n ∈ S v_{n}\in S . Then v n − 1 ∉ S v_{n-1}\notin S , so S − { v n } S-\{v_{n}\} must be a dominating set in P n − { v n , v n − 1 } = P 3 ​ k P_{n}-\{v_{n},v_{n-1}\}=P_{3k} . Since | S − { v n } | = k = γ ⁡ ( P 3 ​ k ) |S-\{v_{n}\}|=k=\gamma(P_{3k}) it follows from the previous argument that S = { v 2 , v 5 , … , v 3 ​ k − 1 , v 3 ​ k + 2 } S=\{v_{2},v_{5},\ldots,v_{3k-1},v_{3k+2}\} . ∎ Theorem 2.5 . If G G is a path on n n vertices, then its dominion is ζ ⁡ ( G ) = { 1 , if ​ n ≡ 0 ( mod 3 ) ( i ) ( n + 2 ) ​ ( n + 11 ) 18 − 1 , if ​ n ≡ 1 ( mod 3 ) ( i ​ i ) ⌈ n 3 ⌉ + 1 , if ​ n ≡ 2 ( mod 3 ) . ( i ​ i ​ i ) \zeta(G)=\begin{cases}1,&\text{if}\ n\equiv 0\pmod{3}\hskip 36.135pt(i)\\ \frac{(n+2)(n+11)}{18}-1,&\text{if}\ n\equiv 1\pmod{3}\hskip 36.135pt(ii)\\ {\lceil\frac{n}{3}\rceil}+1,&\text{if}\ n\equiv 2\pmod{3}.\hskip 36.135pt(iii)\end{cases} Proof. Part (i). This follows directly from Claim 2.4 . Part (iii). Suppose that n = 3 ​ k + 2 n=3k+2 , k ≥ 1 k\geq 1 , and G = P n G=P_{n} . We have γ ⁡ ( G ) = k + 1 \gamma(G)=k+1 and every γ \gamma -set S ⊆ V ⁡ ( G ) S\subseteq V(G) contains exactly one of v n v_{n} , v n − 1 v_{n-1} . From Claim 2.4 we know that exactly one such S S contains v n v_{n} , so our task is to show that exactly ⌈ n 3 ⌉ = k + 1 {\lceil\frac{n}{3}\rceil}=k+1 such S S contain v n − 1 v_{n-1} . Suppose that S S is a γ \gamma -set for G G containing v n − 1 v_{n-1} . By the reasoning in the proof of Claim 2.4 , each 3 3 -set T r = { v 3 ​ r + 1 , v 3 ​ r + 2 , v 3 ​ r + 3 } \displaystyle T_{r}=\{v_{3r+1},v_{3r+2},v_{3r+3}\} , r = 0 , … , k − 1 r=0,\ldots,k-1 , contains exactly one vertex of S S . If 0 ≤ r < k − 1 0\leq r<k-1 and S ∩ T r = { v 3 ​ r + 1 } \displaystyle S\cap T_{r}=\{v_{3r+1}\} then, because S S is dominating in G G , S ∩ T r + 1 = { v 3 ​ r + 4 } S\cap T_{r+1}=\{v_{3r+4}\} ; consequently, if S ∩ T r = { v 3 ​ r + 1 } S\cap T_{r}=\{v_{3r+1}\} then S ∩ T s = { v 3 ​ s + 1 } S\cap T_{s}=\{v_{3s+1}\} , r ≤ s ≤ k − 1 r\leq s\leq k-1 . On the other hand, if S ∩ T r = { v 3 ​ r + 2 } S\cap T_{r}=\{v_{3r+2}\} then S ∩ T r + 1 S\cap T_{r+1} is either { v 3 ​ r + 4 } \{v_{3r+4}\} or { v 3 ​ r + 5 } \{v_{3r+5}\} . Since S ∩ T 0 S\cap T_{0} is either { v 1 } \{v_{1}\} or { v 2 } \{v_{2}\} (since v 3 v_{3} does not cover v 1 v_{1} ), it follows that the γ \gamma -set for G = P n G=P_{n} , n = 3 ​ k + 2 n=3k+2 , containing v n − 1 v_{n-1} are the following: S 0 \displaystyle S_{0} = { v 1 , v 4 , … , v n − 4 , v n − 1 } , \displaystyle=\{v_{1},v_{4},\ldots,v_{n-4},v_{n-1}\}, S 1 \displaystyle S_{1} = { v 2 , v 4 , … , v n − 4 , v n − 1 } , \displaystyle=\{v_{2},v_{4},\ldots,v_{n-4},v_{n-1}\}, S r \displaystyle S_{r} = { v 2 , v 5 , … , v 3 ​ r − 1 , v 3 ​ r + 1 , … , v n − 4 , v n − 1 } , \displaystyle=\{v_{2},v_{5},\ldots,v_{3r-1},v_{3r+1},\ldots,v_{n-4},v_{n-1}\}, S k \displaystyle S_{k} = { v 2 , v 5 , … , v n − 3 , v n − 1 } , \displaystyle=\{v_{2},v_{5},\ldots,v_{n-3},v_{n-1}\}, and there are exactly k + 1 k+1 of these sets. Part(ii) We recursively obtain the ζ \zeta values of G k = P 3 ​ k + 1 := v 1 − v 2 − … − v 3 ​ k − v 3 ​ k + 1 G_{k}=P_{3k+1}:=v_{1}-v_{2}-\ldots-v_{3k}-v_{3k+1} as we increase the value of k ≥ 1 k\geq 1 . For simplicity, let ζ ⁡ ( G k ) = ζ k \zeta(G_{k})=\zeta_{k} , k ≥ 1 k\geq 1 . When k = 1 k=1 , it is easy to verify that the γ \gamma -sets of G 1 G_{1} are { v 1 , v 3 } \{v_{1},v_{3}\} , { v 1 , v 4 } \{v_{1},v_{4}\} , { v 2 , v 3 } \{v_{2},v_{3}\} , { v 2 , v 4 } \{v_{2},v_{4}\} . Hence ζ 1 = 4 \zeta_{1}=4 . When k = 2 k=2 , it is clear that γ ⁡ ( G 2 ) = 3 \gamma(G_{2})=3 . Since v 6 v_{6} covers v 5 v_{5} and v 7 v_{7} , we add v 6 v_{6} to each of the γ \gamma -sets of G 1 G_{1} to get { v 1 , v 3 , v 6 } \{v_{1},v_{3},v_{6}\} , { v 1 , v 4 , v 6 } \{v_{1},v_{4},v_{6}\} , { v 2 , v 3 , v 6 } \{v_{2},v_{3},v_{6}\} , { v 2 , v 4 , v 6 } \{v_{2},v_{4},v_{6}\} . Further, we add v 7 v_{7} to each γ \gamma -set of G 1 G_{1} that includes v 4 v_{4} since v 4 v_{4} covers v 5 v_{5} , and v 7 v_{7} covers v 6 v_{6} . This produces two additional γ \gamma -sets for G 2 G_{2} , namely { v 1 , v 4 , v 7 } , { v 2 , v 4 , v 7 } \{v_{1},v_{4},v_{7}\},\{v_{2},v_{4},v_{7}\} . Finally, with the new and unique set { v 2 , v 5 } \{v_{2},v_{5}\} which covers all other vertices except v 7 v_{7} , we generate two more γ \gamma -sets of G 2 G_{2} using v 6 v_{6} for one and v 7 v_{7} for the other. It is clear that v 6 v_{6} covers v 7 v_{7} (and vice-versa), giving { v 2 , v 5 , v 6 } , { v 2 , v 5 , v 7 } \{v_{2},v_{5},v_{6}\},\{v_{2},v_{5},v_{7}\} . Hence ζ 2 = 8 \zeta_{2}=8 . When k = 3 k=3 , it is also clear that γ ⁡ ( G 3 ) = 4 \gamma(G_{3})=4 . We use a very similar construction as in the case when k = 2 k=2 . Since v 9 v_{9} covers v 8 v_{8} and v 10 v_{10} , we add v 9 v_{9} to each of the 8 8 γ \gamma -sets of G 2 G_{2} to get { v 1 , v 3 , v 6 , v 9 } \{v_{1},v_{3},v_{6},v_{9}\} , { v 1 , v 4 , v 6 , v 9 } \{v_{1},v_{4},v_{6},v_{9}\} , { v 2 , v 3 , v 6 , v 9 } \{v_{2},v_{3},v_{6},v_{9}\} , { v 2 , v 4 , v 6 , v 9 } \{v_{2},v_{4},v_{6},v_{9}\} , { v 1 , v 4 , v 7 , v 9 } , { v 2 , v 4 , v 7 , v 9 } \{v_{1},v_{4},v_{7},v_{9}\},\{v_{2},v_{4},v_{7},v_{9}\} , { v 2 , v 5 , v 6 , v 9 } , { v 2 , v 5 , v 7 , v 9 } \{v_{2},v_{5},v_{6},v_{9}\},\{v_{2},v_{5},v_{7},v_{9}\} . Further, we add v 10 v_{10} to each γ \gamma -set of G 2 G_{2} that includes v 7 v_{7} since v 7 v_{7} covers v 8 v_{8} , and v 10 v_{10} covers v 9 v_{9} . This produces three additional γ \gamma -sets for G 3 G_{3} , namely { v 1 , v 4 , v 7 , v 10 } , { v 2 , v 4 , v 7 , v 10 } , { v 2 , v 5 , v 7 , v 10 } \{v_{1},v_{4},v_{7},v_{10}\},\{v_{2},v_{4},v_{7},v_{10}\},\{v_{2},v_{5},v_{7},v_{10}\} . Finally, with the unique set { v 2 , v 5 , v 8 } \{v_{2},v_{5},v_{8}\} which covers all other vertices except v 10 v_{10} , we generate two more γ \gamma -sets of G 3 G_{3} using v 9 v_{9} for one and v 10 v_{10} for the other. Thus, v 9 v_{9} covers v 10 v_{10} (and vice-versa) giving { v 2 , v 5 , v 8 , v 9 } , { v 2 , v 5 , v 8 , v 10 } \{v_{2},v_{5},v_{8},v_{9}\},\{v_{2},v_{5},v_{8},v_{10}\} . Hence ζ 3 = 13 \zeta_{3}=13 . Now, we generalize the previous constructions for all k ≥ 2 k\geq 2 . It is obvious that γ ⁡ ( G k ) = k + 1 \gamma(G_{k})=k+1 . So, for k ≥ 2 k\geq 2 , we repeatedly form the γ \gamma -sets of G k G_{k} by extending those of G k − 1 G_{k-1} as follows: (a) Since v 3 ​ k v_{3k} covers both v 3 ​ k − 1 v_{3k-1} and v 3 ​ k + 1 v_{3k+1} , we add v 3 ​ k v_{3k} to each γ \gamma -set of G k − 1 G_{k-1} . (b) We add v 3 ​ k + 1 v_{3k+1} to each γ \gamma -set of G k − 1 G_{k-1} that includes v 3 ​ k − 2 v_{3k-2} since v 3 ​ k − 2 v_{3k-2} covers v 3 ​ k − 1 v_{3k-1} , and v 3 ​ k + 1 v_{3k+1} covers v 3 ​ k v_{3k} . (c) Given the unique set { v 2 , v 5 , … , v 3 ​ k − 1 } \{v_{2},v_{5},\ldots,v_{3k-1}\} which covers all other vertices of G k G_{k} except v 3 ​ k + 1 v_{3k+1} , we generate two more γ \gamma -sets of G k G_{k} using v 3 ​ k v_{3k} for one and v 3 ​ k + 1 v_{3k+1} for the other. Clearly v 3 ​ k v_{3k} covers v 3 ​ k + 1 v_{3k+1} (and vice-versa) and we have { v 2 , v 5 , … , v 3 ​ k − 1 , v 3 ​ k } \{v_{2},v_{5},\ldots,v_{3k-1},v_{3k}\} and { v 2 , v 5 , … 