3 ​ k C_{3k} , is one of those 3 3 γ \gamma -sets in C 3 ​ k C_{3k} already discovered. ∎ The result follows from Theorem 2.5 and Claim 2.9 . ∎ Remark 2.10 . The cases when n = 3 ​ k + 1 n=3k+1 and n = 3 ​ k + 2 n=3k+2 turn out to be more difficult to establish and perhaps require a novel technique. However, using Lagrange interpolation on some early recurring ζ \zeta -values, we present a conjecture. Conjecture 2.1 . If G G is a cycle on n ≥ 3 n\geq 3 vertices, then its dominion is ζ ⁡ ( G ) = { n 2 + 5 ​ n 6 , if ​ n ≡ 1 ( mod 3 ) n , if ​ n ≡ 2 ( mod 3 ) . \zeta(G)=\begin{cases}\frac{n^{2}+5n}{6},&\text{if}\ n\equiv 1\pmod{3}\\ n,&\text{if}\ n\equiv 2\pmod{3}.\\ \end{cases} 3 Dominion of Join of Graphs There is no obvious comparative bound between γ ⁡ ( G ) \gamma(G) and ζ ⁡ ( G ) \zeta(G) because, while the former determines the size of the minimum dominating set, the latter counts such sets. Yet, with the few upcoming results, we establish some relationships between these two parameters, in particular using the join operation. Recall, the join of G 1 G_{1} and G 2 G_{2} , commonly denoted by G = G 1 ∨ G 2 G=G_{1}\vee G_{2} , is the graph obtained from the disjoint union of G 1 G_{1} and G 2 G_{2} by adding all edges between V ⁡ ( G 1 ) V(G_{1}) and V ⁡ ( G 2 ) V(G_{2}) . Proposition 3.1 . For any connected graph G G on n n vertices, 1 ≤ ζ ⁡ ( G ) ≤ ( n γ ⁡ ( G ) ) 1\leq\zeta(G)\leq{n\choose\gamma(G)} . These trivial bounds follow from the fact that any γ \gamma -set is some selection from V ⁡ ( G ) V(G) and ζ \zeta counts such sets of size γ ⁡ ( G ) \gamma(G) . The lower bound is tight for stars while the upper bound is tight for complete graphs. Theorem 3.2 . Suppose G 1 G_{1} and G 2 G_{2} are two connected graphs with 2 ≤ γ ⁡ ( G 1 ) ≤ γ ⁡ ( G 2 ) 2\leq\gamma(G_{1})\leq\gamma(G_{2}) . Then ζ ⁡ ( G 1 ∨ G 2 ) ≥ | V ⁡ ( G 1 ) | ⋅ | V ⁡ ( G 2 ) | \zeta(G_{1}\vee G_{2})\geq|V(G_{1})|\cdot|V(G_{2})| . Proof. Suppose G = G 1 ∨ G 2 G=G_{1}\vee G_{2} with 2 ≤ γ ⁡ ( G 1 ) ≤ γ ⁡ ( G 2 ) 2\leq\gamma(G_{1})\leq\gamma(G_{2}) . Consider any pair { u , v } \{u,v\} with u ∈ V ⁡ ( G 1 ) u\in V(G_{1}) , v ∈ V ⁡ ( G 2 ) v\in V(G_{2}) . It follows that it is a γ \gamma -set of G G , since u u covers V ⁡ ( G 2 ) V(G_{2}) and v v covers V ⁡ ( G 1 ) V(G_{1}) , giving the bound. ∎ The previous argument also establishes that 1 ≤ γ ⁡ ( G 1 ∨ G 2 ) ≤ 2 1\leq\gamma(G_{1}\vee G_{2})\leq 2 , given any two graphs G 1 G_{1} , G 2 G_{2} . Theorem 3.3 . Suppose G 1 G_{1} and G 2 G_{2} are two connected graphs with γ ⁡ ( G 1 ) ≤ γ ⁡ ( G 2 ) \gamma(G_{1})\leq\gamma(G_{2}) . Then γ ⁡ ( G 1 ∨ G 2 ) = { 1 if ​ γ ​ ( G 1 ) = 1 2 if ​ γ ​ ( G 1 ) ≥ 2 . \gamma(G_{1}\vee G_{2})=\begin{cases}1&\text{if}\ \gamma(G_{1})=1\\ 2&\text{if}\ \gamma(G_{1})\geq 2.\end{cases} Moreover, ζ ⁡ ( G 1 ∨ G 2 ) = { ζ ⁡ ( G 1 ) + ζ ⁡ ( G 2 ) + { 0 if ​ γ ​ ( G 1 ) = 1 = γ ⁡ ( G 2 ) | V ⁡ ( G 1 ) | ⋅ | V ⁡ ( G 2 ) | , if ​ γ ​ ( G 1 ) = 2 = γ ⁡ ( G 2 ) ζ ⁡ ( G 1 ) + { 0 if ​ γ ​ ( G 1 ) = 1 < γ ⁡ ( G 2 ) | V ⁡ ( G 1 ) | ⋅ | V ⁡ ( G 2 ) | , if ​ γ ​ ( G 1 ) = 2 < γ ⁡ ( G 2 ) | V ⁡ ( G 1 ) | ⋅ | V ⁡ ( G 2 ) | , if ​ 2 < γ ⁡ ( G 1 ) ≤ γ ⁡ ( G 2 ) . \zeta(G_{1}\vee G_{2})=\begin{cases}\zeta(G_{1})+\zeta(G_{2})+\begin{cases}0&\text{if}\ \gamma(G_{1})=1=\gamma(G_{2})\\ |V(G_{1})|\cdot|V(G_{2})|,&\text{if}\ \gamma(G_{1})=2=\gamma(G_{2})\end{cases}\\ \\ \zeta(G_{1})\hskip 39.74872pt+\begin{cases}0&\text{if}\ \gamma(G_{1})=1<\gamma(G_{2})\\ |V(G_{1})|\cdot|V(G_{2})|,&\text{if}\ \gamma(G_{1})=2<\gamma(G_{2})\par\end{cases}\\ \\ |V(G_{1})|\cdot|V(G_{2})|,\hskip 90.3375pt\ \ \text{if}\ 2<\gamma(G_{1})\leq\gamma(G_{2}).\end{cases} Proof. The first part of the statement regarding the values of γ ⁡ ( G 1 ∨ G 2 ) \gamma(G_{1}\vee G_{2}) is trivial. So, we proceed to establish the results in the second part of the statement. Throughout, we denote by S 1 S_{1} and S 2 S_{2} any γ \gamma -sets of G 1 G_{1} and G 2 G_{2} , respectively. (a) Suppose γ ⁡ ( G 1 ) = 1 = γ ⁡ ( G 2 ) \gamma(G_{1})=1=\gamma(G_{2}) . Given the join operation, any vertex v 1 ∈ S 1 v_{1}\in S_{1} also covers any other vertex v 2 ∈ V ⁡ ( G 2 ) v_{2}\in V(G_{2}) . Likewise, any vertex v 2 ∈ S 2 v_{2}\in S_{2} also covers any other vertex v 1 ∈ V ⁡ ( G 1 ) v_{1}\in V(G_{1}) . Since S 1 S_{1} and S 2 S_{2} are any γ \gamma -sets of G 1 G_{1} and G 2 G_{2} , respectively, the result follows. (b) Suppose γ ⁡ ( G 1 ) = 2 = γ ⁡ ( G 2 ) \gamma(G_{1})=2=\gamma(G_{2}) . It is clear that each γ \gamma -set S i S_{i} , i = 1 , 2 , i=1,2, is also a γ \gamma - set of G 1 ∨ G 2 G_{1}\vee G_{2} , following from ( a ) (a) . The result follows the argument presented in the proof of Theorem 3.2 . (c) Suppose γ ⁡ ( G 1 ) < γ ⁡ ( G 2 ) \gamma(G_{1})<\gamma(G_{2}) with γ ⁡ ( G 1 ) ∈ { 1 , 2 } \gamma(G_{1})\in\{1,2\} . It follows that γ ⁡ ( G 1 ∨ G 2 ) = γ ⁡ ( G 1 ) \gamma(G_{1}\vee G_{2})=\gamma(G_{1}) . Further, if γ ⁡ ( G 1 ) = 1 \gamma(G_{1})=1 , then S 1 S_{1} is also a γ \gamma -set of G 1 ∨ G 2 G_{1}\vee G_{2} and S 2 S_{2} is not, since its size is greater than 1. Similarly, if γ ⁡ ( G 1 ) = 2 \gamma(G_{1})=2 , S 1 S_{1} is a γ \gamma -set of G 1 ∨ G 2 G_{1}\vee G_{2} but not S 2 S_{2} . The result follows from the argument presented in the proof of Theorem 3.2 . (d) Finally, suppose 2 < γ ⁡ ( G 1 ) ≤ γ ⁡ ( G 2 ) 2<\gamma(G_{1})\leq\gamma(G_{2}) . It must be that γ ⁡ ( G 1 ∨ G 2 ) = 2 \gamma(G_{1}\vee G_{2})=2 . In which case, neither S 1 S_{1} nor S 2 S_{2} is a γ \gamma -set of G 1 ∨ G 2 G_{1}\vee G_{2} . The result follows from the argument presented in the proof of Theorem 3.2 . ∎ Corollary 3.4 . For any connected graph G G with γ ⁡ ( G ) = 1 \gamma(G)=1 , ζ ⁡ ( G ∨ … ∨ G ⏟ r ) = r ​ ζ ​ ( G ) \zeta(\underbrace{G\vee\ldots\vee G}_{r})=r\zeta(G) , r ≥ 1 r\geq 1 . Proof. From Part (a) of Theorem 3.3 (when γ ⁡ ( G 1 ) = 1 = γ ⁡ ( G 2 ) \gamma(G_{1})=1=\gamma(G_{2}) ), the result follows by induction on r ≥ 1 r\geq 1 . ∎ We note that in the case when G = K 1 G=K_{1} , it is clear that G ∨ … ∨ G ⏟ r := K r